'''Gabrielle Bloch''' (17 February 1870<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.geni.com/people/Gab-Sor%C3%A8re/6000000009087343426|title=Gab Sorère|website=geni.com|date=29 November 1877 }}</ref>{{Better source needed|date=July 2020}} &ndash; 14 July 1961), known professionally as '''Gab Sorère''', was a French art promoter, set designer, mechanical innovator, filmmaker and choreographer of the [[Belle Époque]]. Collaborating with her partner, [[Loïe Fuller]], to explore illusion through [[luminescence]], she produced films and choreographies which moved performance from dancers being lighted to the abstract vision of lights dancing. When Fuller died, Sorère inherited the dance troupe and laboratory of her partner and strove to keep her legacy as a [[visual effects]] artist alive. She continued to produce innovative productions utilizing fluorescence and light into the 1950s.

==Early life==
Gabrielle Bloch was born in Toul, Lorraine, France, on 17 February 1870, and was the privileged daughter of a French banker, Julien Bloch (1843&ndash;1930).{{sfn|Corinne|2012|p=434}} Her mother, Laura (1847&ndash;1925), wrote the book ''Au loin, impressions hindoues'' (1898).{{sfn|Lapeyre|2010}}{{sfn|Fuller|1913|p=255}} She studied at home, reading [[Arthur Schopenhauer|Schopenhauer]] by the age of nine and studying the literature of ancient India by sixteen.{{sfn|Fuller|1913|p=255}} This may have been the trip recorded by her mother in the travelogue, which recounted visits to [[Ceylon]], the [[Himalayas]] and northern India.{{sfn|Lapeyre|2010}} Bloch first saw [[Loïe Fuller]] perform at her Paris debut in 1892, when her mother took her to the performance.{{sfn|Fuller|1913|p=256}} She was familiar with the women in [[Natalie Barney]]'s and [[Gertrude Stein]]'s salons, which included [[Romaine Brooks]], [[Eileen Gray]], and [[Marie-Louise Damien]], a singer better known as Damia, but like Gray.{{sfn|Corinne|2012|p=435}}{{sfn|Fuller|1913|pp=250, 252}}

==Career==
By 1898, Sorère was living with Fuller, stirring controversy for being openly [[lesbian]]; Fuller being eight years older than Bloch; and the latter's penchant for routinely dressing as a man.{{sfn|Coleman|2007|p=173}} During [[World War I]], Bloch established a relief service to transport clothing and food supplies to Belgium and northern France.{{sfn|''The Sebastopol Times''|1917|p=8}} She was instrumental in urging Fuller to open a dance school to prevent her rival [[Isadora Duncan]] from gaining the upper hand with students.{{sfn|Garelick|2009|p=175}} Bloch took the professional name of Gab Sorère around 1920,{{sfn|Coleman|2007|p=173}} and collaborated with Fuller, while working as a promoter of other artists.{{sfn|Townsend|2017|p=151}}{{sfn|Ruprecht|2008|p=232}} Fuller was the performer of the duo and Sorère worked as a [[stage designer]] and invented mechanical props, branching into [[filmmaking]].{{sfn|Ruprecht|2008|p=232}} The two women would make three films together, ''Le Lys de la vie'' (The Lily of Life, 1921), ''Visions des rêves'' (Visions of dreams, 1924) and ''Les Incertitudes de Coppélius'' (Uncertainties of Coppelius, 1927).{{sfn|Garelick|2009|p=56}} ''Le Lys de la vie'' was a silent film, based upon a story written by [[Marie of Romania|Queen Marie of Romania]], a close friend of the couple{{sfn|Garelick|2009|pp=57-58}} and is the only one of the films which survived.{{sfn|Garelick|2009|p=56}}

The only surviving reel of her work is a segment from ''Le Lys de la Vie'', and features a show within a show with classically-costumed figures dancing by the sea, a banquet, royal intrigue, and romance with René Clair featured as a prince on horseback.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.sensesofcinema.com/2003/feature-articles/la_loie/ | title="La Loïe" as Pre-Cinematic Performance – Descriptive Continuity of Movement – Senses of Cinema | date=26 July 2004 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://idnc.library.illinois.edu/?a=d&d=STR19210108.2.41 | title=True Republican 8 January 1921 — Illinois Digital Newspaper Collections }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Coleman |first1=Bud |chapter=The Electric Fairy: The Woman Behind the Apparition of Loie Fuller |pages=311–337 |editor1-last=Marra |editor1-first=Kim |editor2-last=Schanke |editor2-first=Robert |title=Staging Desire: Queer Readings of American Theater History |date=2002 |publisher=University of Michigan Press |isbn=978-0-472-90416-7 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Christout |first1=Marie-Françoise |last2=Palfy |first2=Barbara |title=The Dancing Muse of the Belle Époque |journal=Dance Chronicle |date=1996 |volume=19 |issue=2 |pages=213–216 |doi=10.1080/01472529608569242 |jstor=1567904 }}</ref><ref>[https://sfbaytimes.com/camille-saint-saens-and-la-loie-fuller-at-the-panama-pacific-international-exposition/ Camille Saint-Saëns and La Loïe Fuller at the Panama-Pacific International Exposition]</ref>

When she was not collaborating with Fuller, Sorère ran the furniture gallery and interior decorating salon owned by Eileen Gray. The gallery, known as Jean Désert, was open from 1922 to 1930.{{sfn|Corinne|2012|p=434}} During this time, in 1926, Sorère and Fuller accompanied Queen Marie on a tour of the United States.{{sfn|''The Daily News''|1926|p=105}} The following year, Fuller became ill during the filming of ''Les Incertitudes de Coppélius'' and production was broken off while Sorère nursed her. The film was based upon [[E. T. A. Hoffmann]]'s story, ''[[The Sandman (short story)|The Sandman]]'' and featured the dancers of Fuller's troupe. When she became ill with pneumonia, the dancers were sent on tour to [[Cairo]] and Sorère, who was directing the film, made plans for its completion after their return. Fuller died in 1928 and Sorère inherited both the business and the laboratory where the two women conducted experiments with lighting and paint.{{sfn|''The Evening News''|1928|p=4}} She was protective of Fuller's legacy and was known to sue dancers who misrepresented themselves as having affiliations with Fuller or her dance troupe.{{sfn|''The Manchester Guardian''|1929|p=12}}

After Fuller's death, Sorère became the partner of [[Marie-Louise Damien|Damia]]{{sfn|Corinne|2012|p=434}} and continued to experiment with [[Phosphorescence|phosphorescent salts]] to achieve theatrical [[Chemiluminescence|lighting effects]].{{sfn|Garelick|2009|p=40}}
The 1934 film ''La Féerie des Ballets fantastiques de Loïe Fuller'', produced by George R. Busby, featured choreography by Sorère, who had reconstructed some of Fuller's dances.{{sfn|de la Croix|2012|p=54}} Though the storyline was weak, the film was memorable for the techniques employed to alter dimension and perspective by using rapid elongation and [[foreshortening]].{{sfn|Albright|2016|p=722}} Four years later, in 1938, Sorère produced ''Ballets et Lumières'' with the Mazda company as a tribute to Fuller, using [[blacklight]] and fluorescent paint. Taking well-known Fuller dances, like the ''Fire Dance'' and including new choreography of her own, Sorère was able to make the dancers disappear, leaving the audience with only a vision of the movement of light. Though the application of this technology was Sorère's invention, as Fuller had died before exploring blacklight,{{sfn|Albright|2016|p=721}} critical acclaim for the production and innovation of moving dancers from performing in the light to an abstract performance of lights dancing, was given to Fuller.{{sfn|Albright|2016|p=722}} Sorère continued producing choreographies through the 1950s.{{sfn|Loewel|2012}}

